there are a total of six original dune  novels written by Frank Herbert himself  subsequent books were written by his son  Brian Herbert and Kevin J Anderson now  I've been covering dune on this channel  for a couple of years now and since I've  started covering dune one of the main  things that people have been wanting me  to do this entire time is to rank the  books so guess what you guys are getting  your wish in this video I'll be ranking  the six original Frank Herbert dune  books  in this video I will be ranking frank  herbert's original six doom books we  will save the expanded doom books for  later also keep in mind that these  rankings are based on my own opinion you  may have totally different rankings and  that's fine we will start with number  six before I say what number six is I  should say this I don't believe that any  of these books are bad I think they all  have a purpose but if we are judging  each book on its own then I have to give  the number six spot to Doom Messiah  unlike a lot of people I enjoy doing  Messiah but the fact is this novel works  more as a bridge between dune and  Children of Dune then as a novel on its  own the purpose of Messiah is to  demystify Paul and unday fi Paul in the  readers eye and the book succeeds at  this I think though it doesn't follow  the normal structure of a narrative dune  Messiah gets the number 6 spot because I  feel as though the other five books are  just that much better and expand more on  the dune universe the number 5 spot goes  to chapter house toon chapter house was  originally intended to be the  penultimate novel in the dune saga  things really become desperate in this  book for the Binnie Jesuit sisterhood as  they struggle against the powerful  honored mantras who have unleashed a  devastating assault on the old Empire  chapter house is a great book with a  really memorable ending as well which  people have been debating about for  years number four on my list is heretics  of doom heretics of dune kicks off what  Frank Herbert had intended to be the  final half of the dune saga heretics  introduced us to a new imperial  structure which had formed after the  fall of the worm god emperor in the  later dune books Herbert is exploring  the long view of history this book opens  up nearly 5000 years after the start of  the first novel characters of previous  books have become legends truth has been  distorted by history events that were  witnessed firsthand in previous books  are now myths this book gives us details  about the Bene Gesserit sisterhood that  we had never had before as well and it  was in this novel that I first realized  just how significant the binning Jesuit  powers were they possess the truth  historical records available now that  the worm God had passed their own  ancestral memories stretched back eons  taking the number three spot on my list  is Children of Dune Children of Dune  introduces Leto anga Nima the children  of Paul Atreides who like their aunt  alia had been awakened in the womb this  book also features the return of the  Binney desert lady Jessica mother of  Paul Atreides who featured prominently  in book 1 but was only mentioned in dune  Messiah much of Children of Dune centers  around the mystery of Jack arute - a  mythological siege said to have been  lost to time once it had been home to  the Indu Ali water stealers the most  abominable of all Freeman criminals in  this book Paul's sister Aliyah now in  power struggles with the internal  influence of her maternal grandfather  the Baron Harkonnen Children of Dune is  effectively the end of the first dune  trilogy it ties off most of the hanging  threads of the first two novels and  plants the seeds for the future books  the number 2 spot on my list is Frank  Herbert's classic original book dune I  love doom obviously Frank Herbert's  original book is a science fiction  masterpiece that is influenced countless  novels since I was completely captured  by this universe upon my first reading  of this book it is so impressive how  Frank Herbert builds this gigantic  galaxy in the first book everything just  seems so alive and real dune is an  iconic book what else can I say about it  so it was hard making this list but the  one thing that I was pretty much certain  about was that god emperor of dune would  be number one on my list God Emperor of  dune became my instant favorite when I  first read it I've never quite read  another book like it it is so  unbelievably rich in philosophy and  allegory even more so than the other  novels in this book Frank Herbert shows  us simultaneously how legends and myths  are created and how they evolved over  time and how they are ultimately  destroyed  reading this book feels as though you  are reading records of true events and  the character Leto himself after living  thousands of years on exploring the  multitude of his inner lives is a truly  fascinating and bizarre figure unlike  any other in fiction so there you have  it I have ranked all six books like I  said in the beginning I loved all of  these books even the Brian Herbert ones  and everyone's gonna have a different  opinion but thank you guys so much for  watching and if you liked this video  consider liking and subscribing and if  you really want to support this channel  click the patreon link in the  description
 

